#
# Conformance case: enactment/frontier-labels-are-derived
#
# The actionable frontier is DERIVED, and this case is the proof. Not one
# logic:entryLabel is asserted below: every entry carries only its lifecycle-axis
# witnesses, and the five rules named in profile.json's "shipped_rules" compute the
# label.
#
# Those five rules are NOT restated here. They are resolved out of
# slices/grounding/logic/module.ttl by IRI, so the case reasons with the rule set
# that actually ships and the harness hard-fails on an IRI the module no longer
# declares. A copy of a rule inside this file would keep the case green after the
# shipped rule was deleted — it would pin the copy, which is the one thing a
# derivation corpus must never do.
#
# What the expected materialization must show, per entry:
#
#   readyEntry       StepReady + ApprovalNull      -> FrontierReadyAuthorized
#   gatedEntry       StepReady + ApprovalCreated   -> FrontierReadyApprovalRequired
#   unknownEntry     EffectAttempted               -> FrontierReconciliationRequired
#   receiptedEntry   EffectReceipted               -> FrontierCompensationEligible
#   blockedEntry     StepWaiting + a gap on its    -> FrontierBlockedCapabilityOrResource
#                    action step
#
# Two of these carry the load the rest of the kernel rests on. `unknownEntry` is the
# no-blind-retry law stated POSITIVELY: rather than only forbidding a retry from an
# undetermined position, the frontier names reconciliation as the licensed action, so
# an operator is told what to do instead of only what not to do. `receiptedEntry`
# derives ELIGIBILITY and nothing more — no compensation attempt, no outcome, and no
# claim that compensating will succeed, because compensation is a new action with its
# own possibility of failing.
#
# readyEntry and gatedEntry differ in exactly one axis position, which is the whole
# argument for keeping readiness and approval separate: a single blended state would
# need a combined value for every such pairing.

@prefix rdfs:  <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix logic: <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/> .
@prefix e:     <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/conformance/frontier/> .

# --- The data world: five entries, and NOT ONE logic:entryLabel ----------------

e:readyEntry a logic:FrontierEntry ;
    logic:entryAxisWitness logic:StepReady, logic:ApprovalNull .

e:gatedEntry a logic:FrontierEntry ;
    logic:entryAxisWitness logic:StepReady, logic:ApprovalCreated .

e:unknownEntry a logic:FrontierEntry ;
    logic:entryAxisWitness logic:EffectAttempted .

e:receiptedEntry a logic:FrontierEntry ;
    logic:entryAxisWitness logic:EffectReceipted .

# Waiting alone does not reach a label. The gap standing on this entry's action
# step is what separates "blocked by a capability the deployment does not have"
# from an ordinary wait, and the rule joins through logic:entryAction to find it.
e:blockedEntry a logic:FrontierEntry ;
    logic:entryAction e:ocrStep ;
    logic:entryAxisWitness logic:StepWaiting .

e:ocrStep a logic:TransactionStep ;
    rdfs:label "Extract text"@x-gmeow-english .

# An OPERATIONAL capability gap — a capability the deployment does not HAVE.
# Deliberately not a reasoning-profile gap, which is a claim about what a fragment
# can EXPRESS; the two are different failures with different remedies.
e:ocrGap a logic:OperationalCapabilityGap ;
    logic:gapBlockedStep e:ocrStep .
